        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Frequently asked questions</h2>

        {[
          { q: "Is canvas UGC replacing traditional UGC?", a: "Not replacing — complementing. Traditional UGC remains the best model for paid ad creative and campaign-specific content. Canvas UGC is becoming the default for brands that need always-on organic content at scale. Most brands will eventually use both." },
          { q: "Can traditional UGC creators switch to canvas UGC?", a: "Yes. The core skill — creating authentic, engaging video content — transfers directly. The main adjustment is shifting from 'one polished video per brief' to 'high volume, iterative content' and learning to write hooks that drive organic discovery. Many creators successfully do both simultaneously." },
          { q: "Which model is cheaper?", a: "At small volumes (under 10 videos), traditional UGC is comparable or cheaper. At scale (50+ videos/month), canvas UGC is dramatically cheaper because organic distribution is free. A 100-video/month canvas program costs $3K-$8K in creator payments vs. $15K-$50K for the same volume in traditional UGC." },
          { q: "Do I need an agency for canvas UGC?", a: "Not required, but strongly recommended at scale. Canvas UGC involves account management, creator recruitment, cross-platform tracking, and automated payouts — operational complexity that most brands aren't set up to handle in-house. OKAD manages canvas UGC programs end to end." },
          { q: "What if my canvas content doesn't perform?", a: "That's expected — the model is built on a power law. Only about 1.2% of posts cross 10,000 views. The strategy is volume: produce enough content that the breakout posts generate outsized returns. If no content performs after 60+ posts, the issue is usually niche selection or content quality, not the model itself." },
          { q: "Can I use the same creators for both models?", a: "Yes, but be clear about expectations. Traditional UGC briefs typically require polished, scripted content with revisions. Canvas briefs are looser — frameworks and hooks, not scripts. Some creators excel at both; others have a strong preference for one style." },
          { q: "How does content quality compare?", a: "Traditional UGC is typically more polished (scripted, edited, reviewed). Canvas UGC is typically more native and authentic (shot quickly, minimal editing, feels organic). Both can be high quality — they just optimize for different things. Polished ≠ better performance on social platforms." },
        ].map((item) => (
          <div class="faq-item border-b border-border">
            <div class="faq-q flex justify-between items-center gap-4 py-5 cursor-pointer">
              <span class="font-display font-semibold text-[16px] text-ink">{item.q}</span>
              <span class="font-mono text-[20px] text-accent shrink-0 faq-icon">+</span>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-a overflow-hidden max-h-0 transition-all duration-300">
              <div class="pb-5 text-[15px] text-ink-2 leading-[1.65]">{item.a}</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

